<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=glassblowing">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glassblowing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as glass blowing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Glassblowing is the technique of inflating molten glass into a bubble with a blowpipe and shaping it, developed by Phoenician and Syrian craftsmen in the 1st century BCE, in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and Israel. It quickly eclipsed casting and core-forming, spreading with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> to workshops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and beyond. Mould-blowing, introduced in the 1st century CE, allowed glass vessels to be made quickly in large quantities. Free-blowing remained the leading way of forming glass until the late 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glassblowing.jpg" alt="Glassblowing" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Le_ma%C3%AEtre_verrier_Werner_Garcia_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
